readings.c1s1:
¶
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To lash out, as a horse; kick. [Obsolete or provincial.]
{{smaller block|I holde him not for a good beast that when they lade him will stand stock stil, and when they unlade him will ''yerke'' out behinde.
{{right|''Guevara'', Letters (tr. by Hellowes, 1577), p. 81.}}
The horse, being mad withal, ''yerked'' out behind.
{{right|''North.''}}}}
'''2.''' To move with sudden jerks; jerk. [Obsolete or provincial.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Skud from the lashes of my ''yerking'' rime.</poem>
{{right|''Marston'', Scourge of Villanie, i., Prol.}}}}

'''yerk'''<sup>2</sup> (yėrk), ''n.'' [< ''yerk''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.''] A sudden or quick thrust or motion; a kick; a smart stroke; a blow. Also ''yark.'' [Obsolete or provincial.]
{{smaller block|A ''yarke'' of a whip. {{right|''Florio'', p. 98.}}
Imagine twenty thousand of them . . . battering the warriors’ faces into mummy by terrible ''yerks'' from their hinder hoofs.
{{right|''Swift'', Gulliver’s Travels, iv. 12.}}}}

'''yerl''' (yėrl), ''n.'' A Scotch form of ''earl.''

'''yern'''<sup>1</sup>†, ''v. i.'' An old spelling of ''yearn''<sup>1</sup>.

'''yern'''<sup>1</sup>†, ''a.'' [ME., < AS. ''georn'', eager: see ''yearn''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] Brisk; lively; sprightly; eager.
{{smaller block|<poem>But of hir song it was as loud and ''yerne''
As any swalwe sittynge on a berne.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 71.}}}}

'''yern'''<sup>2</sup>†, ''v. i.'' [ME. ''ȝirnen'', ''ȝernen'', < AS. ''geyrnan'', ''geærnan'', run, tr. run for, gain by running, < ''ge-'' + ''yrnan'', ''ærnan'', run: see ''run''<sup>1</sup>, ''ren''<sup>1</sup>, and cf. ''earn''<sup>2</sup>, ''yearn''<sup>3</sup>.] To run; pass swiftly.
{{smaller block|<poem>Thus ''ȝirnez'' the ȝere in ȝisterdayez mony,</poem>}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|While we were ''yet'' sinners, Christ died for us. Rom. v. 8.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I see him ''yet'', the princely boy!</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L., ii. 32.}}}}
'''4.''' At or before some future time; before all is done.
{{smaller block|Hope thou in God; for I shall ''yet'' praise him. Ps. xlii. 11.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>He’ll be hanged ''yet'',
Though every drop of water
. . . gape . . . to glut him.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, i. 1. 61.}}}}
'''5.''' Up to the present time; thus far; hitherto; already: usually with a negative.
{{smaller block|The Holy Ghost was not ''yet'' given; because that Jesus was not ''yet'' glorified. John vii. 39.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Let me remember thee what thou hast promised,
Which is not ''yet'' performed me.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, i. 2. 244.}}}}
{{smaller block|Opportunity hath baulked them ''yet''.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Every Man in his Humour, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|The Iland, not ''yet'' Britain but Albion, was in a manner desert and inhospitable. ''Milton'', Hist. Eng., i.}}
''Yet'' is often accompanied by ''as'' in this sense: as, I have not met him ''as yet''.
{{smaller block|Unreconciled ''as yet'' to heaven. ''Shak.'', Othello, v. 2. 72.}}
'''6.''' Though the case be such; at least; at any rate.
{{smaller block|<poem>Madam, if your heart be so obdurate,
Vouchsafe me ''yet'' your picture for my love.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., iv. 2. 121.}}}}
{{smaller block|An unhappy François who, after passing eighteen years in prison, ''yet'' won the grace and love of Joan of Naples by his charms. ''J. A. Symonds'', Italy and Greece, p. 330.}}
''Yet'' is sometimes used with adjectives or participles (with or without a hyphen) to denote continuance of the action or state, or as equivalent to ''still''.

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|<poem>He rose, and saw the field deform’d with blood,
An empty space where late the coursers stood,
The ''yet''-warm Thracians panting on the coast.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, x. 612.}}
<poem>Lavaine
Returning brought the ''yet''-unblazon’d shield.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Lancelot and Elaine.}}}}

'''II.''' ''conj.'' '''1.''' Nevertheless; notwithstanding.
{{smaller block|He restored the chief butler unto his butlership again; . . . ''yet'' did not the chief butler remember Joseph, but forgat him.
{{right|Gen. xl. 23.}}
<poem>Blasted, and burnt, and blinded as I was, . . .
O, ''yet'' methought I saw the Holy Grail.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Holy Grail.}}}}
'''2.''' Though.
{{smaller block|I cannot speak to her, ''yet'' she urged conference.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, i. 2. 270.}}}}
'''3.''' But.
{{smaller block|<poem>“No, no,” quoth she, “sweet Death, I did but jest;
''Yet'', pardon me, I felt a kind of fear.”</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 998.}}
Many perished raving mad, fancying themselves swimming in boundless seas, ''yet'' unable to assuage their thirst.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 45.}}}}

'''yet'''<sup>2</sup> (yet), ''v. t.'' [Sc. also ''yit''; < ME. ''yeten'', ''ʒeten'', < AS. ''geótan'', pour: see ''gush''.] To melt; found; cast, as metal. [Obsolete or provincial.]
{{smaller block|To ''ʒett''; fundere, fusare.
{{right|''Cath. Ang.'', p. 426.}}
''ʒetynge'' of metelle, as bellys, pannys, potys, and other lyke.
{{right|''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 538.}}}}
